course: biology
lesson: 108
title: The South American area: sloths, anteaters, piranhas and giant otters
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: A two-toed sloth hangs upside down from a branch and does not seem to move for an hour; it is not lazy but thrifty, because its leaves give so little energy that it saves every bit by moving slowly and keeping its body cool.
explain: Its fur is even a little garden: tiny green algae grow in it and moths live in it, so a still, greenish sloth is hard to spot among the leaves.
explain: Next door a giant anteater walks on its knuckles to keep its long claws sharp, then flicks a sticky tongue about 60 centimetres long into a termite feeder up to about 150 times a minute, without a single tooth in its mouth.
explain: Films show piranhas stripping a cow in seconds, Flash the sloth in Zootopia takes forever to laugh at a joke, and Sid of Ice Age meets dinosaurs; the truth is shy piranhas, sloths that really are slow, and giant ground sloths that lived long after the dinosaurs were gone.
fact: Sloths spend almost all their lives hanging upside down and move very slowly to save energy, because leaves give little of it, and they climb down only about once a week to go to the toilet.
fact: Tiny algae grow in sloth fur and turn it green, which helps hide the sloth, moths live in the fur too, and a sloth can hold its breath for up to about 40 minutes by slowing its heart.
fact: A two-toed sloth has two claws on each front foot and three on each back foot.
fact: Giant anteaters have no teeth; a sticky tongue about 60 centimetres long flicks up to about 150 times a minute, they feed quickly at each nest to avoid too many bites, and the stomach grinds the food.
fact: Giant anteaters walk on their knuckles to protect long front claws, which rip open hard termite mounds and can fight off a jaguar.
fact: Zoos can mix capybaras, rheas, tapirs and anteaters, which live together in the wild and eat different foods; capybaras, the biggest rodents in the world, are so calm that birds and other animals sit on them.
fact: Piranhas are mostly shy fish that eat fish, seeds, insects and dead animals; attacks on people are rare and usually small bites, and they swim in groups mostly for safety from their own predators.
fact: Giant otters from the Amazon grow up to about 1.8 metres long, live in noisy families that hunt fish together and defend their river from caimans and jaguars, and were hunted almost to extinction for their fur.
fact: Rheas are big flightless birds of South America, cousins of ostriches and emus, and a rhea father sits on eggs laid by several females, sometimes over 50.
fact: Llamas and alpacas were bred from wild guanacos and vicunas in the Andes thousands of years ago, llamas to carry loads and alpacas for soft wool, and llamas spit when annoyed, mostly at other llamas.
fact: Some rainforest poison frogs carry their tadpoles on their backs up to little pools in bromeliad plants, safe from fish, and some mothers come back to feed them unfertilised eggs, because the tiny pool has almost no other food.
fact: Sid in Ice Age is a ground sloth; giant ground sloths, some as big as elephants, walked the Americas until about 10,000 years ago, and scientists think people hunting and a changing climate helped wipe them out.
exercise: Why does a sloth move so slowly? | Its leaf food gives little energy, so it saves energy | It is too lazy to bother moving | Its legs are too weak to hold it | It is always half asleep from eating too much ripe fruit
exercise: How often does a sloth climb down from its tree? | About once a week, to go to the toilet | Every hour, to drink | Never in its whole life | Every night, to sleep safely on the warm ground
exercise: What makes a sloth's fur green? | Tiny algae growing in it | Green paint from the keepers | Leaves it chews and rubs in | Its skin colour showing through
exercise: How does green fur help a sloth? | It hides the sloth among leaves | It keeps the sloth warm at night | It tastes bad to jaguars | It helps the sloth make food
exercise: How long can a sloth hold its breath? | Up to about 40 minutes | About 10 seconds | Exactly one day | Sloths cannot hold their breath
exercise: How long is a giant anteater's tongue? | About 60 centimetres | About 6 centimetres | About 6 metres | About as long as a pencil
exercise: Why does an anteater eat only a little at each nest? | To avoid too many bites from ants and termites | Because it is full very fast | To leave plenty of the food behind for its babies | Because its tongue gets tired
exercise: How does an anteater manage without teeth? | Its stomach grinds the food | It swallows the ants alive and whole forever | It has teeth on its tongue | It only eats soft fruit instead
exercise: Why does a giant anteater walk on its knuckles? | To protect its long front claws | Because its feet are always sore | To dig as it walks | To look bigger to jaguars
exercise: Why can capybaras, rheas and tapirs share a paddock? | They live together in the wild and eat different foods | They are all kinds of giant rodent | The keepers make them all sleep at different times of day | They are too slow to hurt each other
exercise: What do piranhas mostly eat? | Fish, seeds, insects and dead animals | Only cows that cross rivers | Only people who swim | Only water plants
exercise: Why do piranhas swim in groups? | Mostly for safety from their own predators | To hunt cows together | To keep the water warm | To find a way out of the river and into the sea
exercise: How do giant otters protect their river? | Their family groups defend it from caimans and jaguars | They hide under rocks all day | They build big dams of sticks and mud across it, like beavers | They bite holes in fishing boats
exercise: What is special about how rhea eggs are cared for? | The father sits on eggs laid by several females | Each mother buries her own eggs deep in the hot sand | The eggs hatch in the river | Nobody looks after them at all
exercise: Which wild animals were llamas and alpacas bred from? | Guanacos and vicunas | Camels and horses | Sheep and goats | Deer and antelopes
exercise: Why do some poison frogs carry tadpoles up to bromeliad pools? | Small pools high in plants are safe from fish | The tadpoles need to be near the sun to grow | The frogs live only in the treetops | It keeps the tadpoles away from the rain
exercise: What kind of animal is Sid from Ice Age? | A ground sloth | A two-toed tree sloth | An anteater | A giant otter
test: A two-toed sloth has 2 claws on each front foot and 3 on each back foot. How many claws on 2 sloths? | 20 | 8 | 12 | 16
test: «piranhas can eat a whole cow in like 5 seconds, saw it in a film» What is true? | Piranhas are mostly shy, and attacks are rare small bites | Yes, they do it every single day to cows crossing the Amazon | Yes, but only to cows, never people | No, piranhas only eat grass
test: In Zootopia, Flash the sloth works at painfully slow speed. How right is that? | Sloths really are very slow, to save energy | Wrong, real sloths are among the fastest animals | Wrong, sloths are fast but sleepy | Wrong, sloths cannot move at all
test: In Ice Age 3, Sid meets dinosaurs. What is wrong? | Ground sloths lived millions of years after the dinosaurs died out | Sloths are dinosaurs themselves | Dinosaurs still lived in the Ice Age | Nothing, ground sloths lived with dinosaurs
test: «that sloth's covered in mould, gross, it's sick» What is right? | It is algae that hides the sloth and is normal | Yes, it is very ill and needs to see a vet straight away | Yes, keepers forgot to wash it | No, that is paint for the visitors
test: 3 rheas and 2 capybaras. How many legs? | 14 | 10 | 20 | 12
test: What would happen if a jaguar attacked a giant anteater? | The anteater could stand up and fight back with its claws | The anteater would bite it hard with its long sharp front teeth | The anteater would climb a tree to escape | The anteater would always be eaten at once
test: In The Emperor's New Groove, a llama spits at people all the time. What is real? | Llamas spit when annoyed, mostly at other llamas | Llamas spit at every person they meet | Llamas never spit at all | Only alpacas spit, never llamas
test: A rhea father sits on 52 eggs from 4 females, the same number from each. How many eggs did each female lay? | 13 | 12 | 48 | 26
test: «giant otters? never heard of 'em, must be loads» Why are they rare? | They were hunted almost to extinction for their fur | They have always been rare in every river of South America | Piranhas ate most of them | They moved to Africa
test: Why would a frog bother to feed its tadpoles its own eggs in a bromeliad pool? | The tiny pool has almost no other food | The eggs keep the water warm | It is getting rid of bad eggs | Tadpoles only like eggs from other frogs
test: «capybaras are just giant guinea pigs with birds on top lol» What is true? | They are huge, calm rodents that let birds sit on them | They are a kind of wild pig that lives in rivers and swamps | They are a kind of small hippo | They carry birds to keep warm
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the slowness of the sloth? | It conserves the scant energy furnished by a diet of leaves | It results from chronic illness caused by the algae in its fur | It is a habit of idleness without biological purpose | It is acquired only in captivity
exam: Identify the means by which the giant anteater, being without teeth, reduces its food. | The food is ground in the stomach | The food is chewed by ridges upon the tongue | The food is dissolved in the mouth before swallowing | The food is softened by soaking in water
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the piranha. | It is in the main a timid fish, and attacks upon man are rare and generally slight | It customarily consumes entire cattle in a matter of seconds when they cross rivers | It feeds solely upon human beings | It is a herbivore consuming nothing but grass
exam: State the period during which giant ground sloths inhabited the Americas, relative to the extinction of the dinosaurs. | Until about ten thousand years ago, long after the dinosaurs had died out | At the same time as the dinosaurs, and no later | Before the dinosaurs had appeared | They inhabit the Americas at the present day
